<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armand-Emmanuel du Plessis, Duc de Richelieu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1766">1766</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Duke of Richelieu, Duc de Richelieu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Armand-Emmanuel du Plessis, Duc de Richelieu, was a French aristocrat who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> during the emigration as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>. Returning after 1814, he was prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Restoration from 1815 to 1818 and from 1820 to 1821. He negotiated the payment of war indemnities and secured the end of the allied occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armand-emmanuel-du-plessis-duc-de-richelieu.jpg" alt="Armand-Emmanuel du Plessis, Duc de Richelieu" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armand_Emmanuel_Duke_of_Richelieu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 25 September 1766, the son of Antoine de Vignerot du Plessis, 4th Duke of Richelieu, and grandson of the favourite of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>. As a boy he was styled Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a>. On 4 May 1782, at fifteen, he was married to Alexandrine Rosalie Sabine de Rochechouart-Faudoas, then fourteen, and left at once on the Grand Tour with his tutor, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. The couple were never more than formal with each other.</p><p>After his travels he entered Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>'s Regiment of Dragoons and took his grandfather's place at court as a premier gentilhomme de la chambre to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. On 5 October 1789, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-march-on-versailles">March on Versailles</a> began, he made his way there on foot, in disguise, and urged the Queen to take refuge in the King's apartments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1790 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on the Queen's behalf. With Prince Charles de Ligne and the Comte de Langeron he joined the Russian army as a volunteer and was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a>'s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izmail">Izmail</a>, for which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> gave him the Order of St. George and a golden sword. He succeeded as Duke of Richelieu in February 1791, served briefly at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries Palace</a>, then joined the émigré army of the Prince of Condé.</p><p>Later he entered Russian service and rose to major general, resigning after a reprimand from Emperor Paul I. After Paul's murder in 1801 his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> had his name struck from the list of prohibited émigrés, and in 1803 appointed him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>. Two years later he became Governor-General of New Russia, which included Kherson, Ekaterinoslav and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. During his eleven years of administration Odessa grew into the third largest city of the empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Richelieu returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1814. He refused a place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a>'s ministry, but after Talleyrand resigned he succeeded him on 26 September 1815 as President of the Council, also holding the foreign ministry, until 29 December 1818. He worked to end the Allied occupation, attending the Congress of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1818, and left office when his colleagues refused to support a change to the electoral law.</p><p>After the murder of the Duke of Berry he returned to the premiership on 21 February 1820, but attacks from the Ultras on one side and the Liberals on the other made his position untenable, and he resigned on 12 December 1821. He died of a stroke on 17 May 1822. The people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> put up a bronze monument to him in 1828.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armand Emmanuel de Vignerot du Plessis, 5th Duke of Richelieu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moderate ministers such as the <b>Duke of Richelieu</b> paid off war indemnities and ended the allied occupation in 1818, while ultra-royalists pressed for revenge after the White Terror.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-restoration">Bourbon Restoration</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Armand-Emmanuel du Plessis, Duc de Richelieu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
